  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/.... \nThe weather pattern for most of next week will see the subtropical \nhigh becoming the dominate feature across the Southwestern U.S. \nwhile its strength will barely change. Guidance agrees the ridge \ncenter will shift from Texas on Monday to somewhere over Arizona by \nWednesday with H5 heights rising to 595-597dm. H5 heights of this \nmagnitude will represent the 97th percentile of climatology for the \nperiod and temperatures will follow with forecast highs only falling \nshort of daily records by a few degrees each day. Since moisture \nlevels are now expected to remain fairly limited for much of, if not \nall of, next week, the day-to-day convective potential should remain \nquite low and likely confined to far eastern Arizona. The most \nlikely scenario keeps the ridge in full control over the Desert \nSouthwest with the center hovering somewhere over Arizona and/or \ninto western New Mexico.\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence for a more prolonged heat event \nleading to the now Extreme Heat Warning covering all lower deserts \nand extending through Friday. NBM forecast highs show readings \nanywhere from 108-113 degrees across the lower deserts each day next \nweek with overnight lows near the 90 degree mark by Tuesday-\nWednesday as surface dew points shift a bit higher. Forecast Major \nHeatRisk has also expanded more across the western lower deserts \ngoing into the middle part of next week with the bulk of the area \nkeeping Major HeatRisk levels through at least next Friday. There is \nuncertainty in the length of time this heat event will last as some \nof the guidance shows the ridge center may eventually shift more to \nthe north and/or east. If this occurs, it should help to increase \nthe amount of monsoon activity over the region, eventually leading \nto some degree of cooling.",
